# API Development Guidelines

## HttpClient Usage

### Current API Structure

- All requests go to **Orders API** (`https://orders.verbit.co/api/v2`)
- HttpClient is simplified with specific methods only
- No generic wrapper methods - only used methods are kept

### Available Methods

```typescript
// Collections
HttpClient.getCollections(params?)
HttpClient.getCollection(id)

// Sessions
HttpClient.getSessions(params?)
HttpClient.getSession(id)
HttpClient.getSessionsByCollection(collectionId, params?)

// Utility methods
HttpClient.get(endpoint, params?)
HttpClient.post(endpoint, data?)
HttpClient.patch(endpoint, data?)
HttpClient.delete(endpoint)
```

### Adding New Endpoints

When adding new API endpoints, follow this pattern:

```typescript
static async createCollection(data: CreateCollectionData): Promise<Collection> {
  return this.request<Collection>({
    ...this.getDefaultConfig(),
    method: 'POST',
    url: '/collections',
    data,
  })
}
```

## Query Hooks Pattern

### React Query Integration

```typescript
// Good pattern - specific hooks
export const useCollectionsQuery = (params?: CollectionsParams) => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: [COLLECTIONS_QUERY_KEY, params],
    queryFn: () => HttpClient.getCollections(params),
    retry: 2,
  })
}
```

### Error Handling

- HttpClient automatically handles errors
- Use React Query's error states
- Global error listeners available via `HttpClient.addErrorListener()`
- **Always use HTTP status constants from `@/constants/httpStatusCodes`**

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use constants for status codes
import {
  AUTHENTICATION_ERROR_STATUS_CODE,
  HTTP_STATUS,
} from '@/constants/httpStatusCodes'

HttpClient.addErrorListener((error) => {
  if (error.response?.status === AUTHENTICATION_ERROR_STATUS_CODE) {
    // Handle 401
  }
  if (error.response?.status === HTTP_STATUS.FORBIDDEN) {
    // Handle 403
  }
})

// ❌ Bad: Magic numbers
if (error.response?.status === 401) {
  // Don't use magic numbers
}
```

## Best Practices

### ✅ Do:

- Use specific HttpClient methods for known endpoints
- Handle errors at the component level with React Query
- Keep query keys consistent and hierarchical
- Use TypeScript interfaces for all API data

### ❌ Don't:

- Create generic wrapper methods unless actually used
- Handle errors globally unless necessary
- Mix different API endpoints in same methods
- Skip type definitions for API responses
